{{also|high falutin}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2008|October|15}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|hifalutin}}, {{l|en|highfaluting}}, {{l|en|high falutin}}

===Etymology===
Alteration of {{m|en|high-fluting}}. In his book, ''The Adventure of English'', Melvyn Bragg records that in a nascent America, when the well-to-do travelled by steamboat, said passengers were referred to as highfalutin due to the high fluted funnels on the boats. Compare [[riffraff]]. Another speculation connects the term with [[high]]-[[flighting]]/-[[flying]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˌhaɪ.fəˈlu.tɨn/|/ˈhaɪ.fəˌlu.tɨn/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-highfalutin.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} Self-[[important]], [[pompous]]; [[arrogant]] or [[egotistical]].
#: {{syn|en|bombast|hoity-toity|pretentious}}
#: {{ux|en|It's only a matter of time before some '''highfalutin''' developer builds a huge hotel and ruins the scenery.}}
#: {{ux|en|That one Cajun that moved to Austin is too '''highfalutin''' to come back to Livingston Parish. He's over there with that mean bread lady!}}
#: {{ux|en|His speech was very '''highfalutin'''.}}

====Usage notes====
Said of people and writing.

